1
00:00:24,420 --> 00:02:14,540
 She knew, for example, in Venezuela, in the USA, in the Humboldt, Greenland, Mount Hum, and New Caledonia.

2
00:02:15,420 --> 00:02:22,620
 Yeah, he was more than an explorer.

3
00:02:22,940 --> 00:02:27,440
 He was an ecologist, someone who observes and tries to understand the harmony and the forces of nature.

4
00:02:27,560 --> 00:02:28,640
 A humanist as well.

5
00:02:28,640 --> 00:02:30,970
 He would defend human rights at all times.

6
00:02:31,130 --> 00:02:39,030
 Mind if I continue?

7
00:02:39,660 --> 00:02:44,530
 So much the Humboldt story.

8
00:02:44,670 --> 00:02:45,510
 Where do I begin?

9
00:02:46,070 --> 00:02:46,960
 I'll start at the beginning.

10
00:02:48,150 --> 00:02:50,420
 This is Paris in the year 1798.

11
00:03:00,560 --> 00:03:08,420
 Monsieur, I wasn't paying attention.

12
00:03:08,740 --> 00:03:09,940
 Entirely my fault, monsieur.

13
00:03:10,120 --> 00:03:10,920
 I was thinking.

14
00:03:11,160 --> 00:03:13,100
 Permit me to introduce myself.

15
00:03:13,200 --> 00:03:14,840
 Alexander von Humboldt from Berlin.

16
00:03:15,020 --> 00:03:16,780
 Et mes bons plans, La Rochelle.

17
00:03:16,960 --> 00:03:17,460
 How do you do?

18
00:03:17,620 --> 00:03:18,800
 You are a botanist.

19
00:03:19,050 --> 00:03:19,500
 A doctor.

20
00:03:19,960 --> 00:03:21,620
 But plants interest me.

21
00:03:22,210 --> 00:03:24,130
 And you appear to be an astronomer.

22
00:03:24,770 --> 00:03:26,160
 Well, not exactly.

23
00:03:26,780 --> 00:03:28,580
 I'm interested in a great deal.

24
00:03:28,780 --> 00:03:29,580
 In all of nature.

25
00:03:30,160 --> 00:03:31,140
 In all of nature.

26
00:03:31,390 --> 00:03:33,090
 I think we might work together.

27
00:03:34,770 --> 00:03:38,460
 It would be useful to compare notes.

28
00:03:38,540 --> 00:03:40,100
 Maybe we could go together on an expedition.

29
00:03:40,240 --> 00:03:40,600
 Why not?

30
00:03:40,850 --> 00:03:41,440
 It's true.

31
00:03:41,440 --> 00:03:43,180
 The world's full of so much to discover.

32
00:03:43,440 --> 00:03:43,560
 Plants.

33
00:03:43,560 --> 00:03:44,960
 Yes, the very laws of nature.

34
00:03:45,080 --> 00:03:45,780
 Human beings.

35
00:03:47,460 --> 00:04:06,580
 Ah, His Majesty Carlos IV, King of Spain, Baron Alexander von Humboldt of Berlin, scholar and philosopher, and Monsieur Aimé Bonpland, French botanist.

36
00:04:07,090 --> 00:04:11,140
 Gentlemen, you wish to explore our colonies out in America. Well, why is that?

37
00:04:11,240 --> 00:04:16,800
 For pure science, Your Majesty. In these regions, our plants and animals never cataloged. Whole peoples are known.

38
00:04:17,060 --> 00:04:20,680
 Only a Frenchman, Monsieur de la Condamine, published a book about the area.

39
00:04:21,350 --> 00:04:25,090
 These days, expeditions are costly, and we're not rich.

40
00:04:25,250 --> 00:04:29,190
 But sire, the Baron von Humboldt is quite prepared to finance the expedition entirely

41
00:04:29,190 --> 00:04:32,710
 and graciously, without charge, give everything he collects to our museums.

42
00:04:33,210 --> 00:04:36,270
 Oh, in that case, well, may I wish you a happy voyage.

43
00:04:36,510 --> 00:04:39,990
 I will instruct all my governors and prefects and captains and, well, you know,

44
00:04:40,210 --> 00:04:41,990
 to assist you in any way they can.

45
00:04:42,230 --> 00:04:45,550
 But be warned, such places are very dangerous.

46
00:04:49,510 --> 00:05:03,740
 Someone here in the prime of life, admire the man's teeth.

47
00:05:05,900 --> 00:05:09,630
 Slavery's wrong. People have a right to be free.

48
00:05:09,630 --> 00:05:14,550
 I know that one day South America will follow the example of North America and Europe.

49
00:05:14,910 --> 00:05:16,970
 There's going to be a revolution down here.

50
00:05:17,170 --> 00:05:18,150
 Do you really think so?

51
00:05:18,320 --> 00:05:20,880
 The Spanish are in power here and they won't give it out.

52
00:05:21,740 --> 00:05:23,020
 You are right, senores.

53
00:05:23,280 --> 00:05:25,040
 One day we will get our liberty.

54
00:05:25,360 --> 00:05:26,520
 You can't speak our language?

55
00:05:26,740 --> 00:05:27,260
 Yes, senor.

56
00:05:27,340 --> 00:05:28,820
 Also many Indian dialects.

57
00:05:29,300 --> 00:05:32,720
 I know the area very well and I'm an expert at paddling canoes.

58
00:05:33,650 --> 00:05:37,190
 Are you interested in working for us, joining our expedition?

59
00:05:37,630 --> 00:05:38,470
 Yes, I am.

60
00:05:38,750 --> 00:05:39,610
 Where are you headed?

61
00:05:39,850 --> 00:05:40,590
 To the south.

62
00:05:40,670 --> 00:05:42,730
 Up the Orinoco, all the way to the Amazon.

63
00:05:43,080 --> 00:05:45,220
 Wait, come over here, to where we can talk.

64
00:05:45,940 --> 00:05:48,330
 First, we have to cross the plains on horseback.

65
00:05:48,630 --> 00:05:50,710
 Then, we must find a dugout canoe to buy.

66
00:05:51,150 --> 00:05:52,260
 What is your name?

67
00:05:52,550 --> 00:05:53,550
 My name is Carlos.

68
00:05:53,910 --> 00:05:55,450
 If you want, we can leave to Morisana.

69
00:05:59,450 --> 00:06:08,610
 Careful, it could be poisonous.

70
00:06:10,020 --> 00:06:12,100
 It's a milk treat, delicious, not poison.

71
00:06:12,860 --> 00:06:20,350
 I wouldn't drink that.

72
00:06:20,530 --> 00:06:23,140
 If the Indians always drink it, why can't I?

73
00:06:23,260 --> 00:06:45,710
 Careful of him, senor. It is quite dangerous. It will shoot lightning bolts so big they will kill a horse, senor.

74
00:06:45,710 --> 00:06:47,310
 No more Carlos. Kill a horse?

75
00:06:47,310 --> 00:06:49,010
 Si, senor. I saw one do it.

76
00:06:49,010 --> 00:06:51,540
 Then I'll have to examine it. This animal is unique.

77
00:06:51,540 --> 00:06:53,790
 Now, wait a minute. I have an idea.

78
00:06:53,790 --> 00:06:56,890
 Hola, amigo. Paella bonia buscada, ma horse.

79
00:06:56,890 --> 00:06:57,830
 Huh?

80
00:06:57,830 --> 00:07:16,670
 This is really extraordinary. An electric shock.

81
00:07:16,670 --> 00:07:19,230
 Look there. That horse is practically dead.

82
00:07:19,230 --> 00:07:30,320
 You see, this organ builds up an electrical charge.

83
00:07:30,320 --> 00:07:33,820
 It's a powerful weapon for this strange creature.

84
00:07:33,820 --> 00:07:46,080
 Bells there are noon. Must have one.

85
00:07:46,080 --> 00:08:05,570
 Mucho dinero para todos.

86
00:08:05,570 --> 00:08:15,260
 This river is the Apurri. It will lead to the Orinoco. It will take a few days.

87
00:08:15,260 --> 00:08:28,190
 These trees are gigantic. It's hard to believe.

88
00:08:28,190 --> 00:08:30,730
 The Orinoco, you see it? There!

89
00:08:30,730 --> 00:08:33,280
 Welcome, welcome!

90
00:08:33,280 --> 00:08:54,140
 Hello, I'm Brother Zeer. You're looking for a canal linking the Orinoco to the Rio Negro, which will bring you to the Amazon.

91
00:08:54,140 --> 00:08:57,550
 Condamine in his early exploration said that there was a canal.

92
00:08:57,550 --> 00:08:59,550
 Brother Alonso went up the Orinoco.

93
00:08:59,550 --> 00:09:00,190
 Mm-hmm.

94
00:09:00,190 --> 00:09:02,190
 He assures me there can be no such canal.

95
00:09:02,190 --> 00:09:03,310
 Well, that we shall see.

96
00:09:03,310 --> 00:09:06,050
 In any case, you won't go far in that nutshell there.

97
00:09:06,050 --> 00:09:10,850
 You will need a much larger boat, a sturdier one, and porters.

98
00:09:10,850 --> 00:09:14,170
 And if you like, I will be glad to accompany you on your journey.

99
00:09:14,170 --> 00:09:18,970
 But now you must come to the monastery and take your refreshment.

100
00:09:18,970 --> 00:09:46,560
 To with the Indians, there are some fish hooks, some leaves of tobacco,

101
00:09:46,560 --> 00:09:49,370
 two small cakes of alcohol, and two more.

102
00:09:49,370 --> 00:09:53,190
 No, no, no, no.

103
00:09:53,190 --> 00:10:17,660
 Oh, my, oh, my rapid.

104
00:10:17,660 --> 00:11:08,030
 An animal.

105
00:11:08,030 --> 00:11:19,230
 Sure be better that way.

106
00:11:19,230 --> 00:11:19,930
 No!

107
00:11:19,930 --> 00:11:21,990
 The boat is just a few feet away.

108
00:11:21,990 --> 00:11:23,490
 It's much simpler than I get.

109
00:11:23,490 --> 00:11:37,600
 Piranha fish, eat up all.

110
00:11:37,600 --> 00:11:39,090
 You lucky this time.

111
00:11:39,090 --> 00:11:58,170
 Quite a specimen.

112
00:11:58,170 --> 00:12:00,240
 Almost ten feet long, that one.

113
00:12:00,240 --> 00:12:42,620
 Casquiare Canal.

114
00:12:42,620 --> 00:12:44,700
 The one that Condamine wrote about.

115
00:12:44,700 --> 00:12:45,980
 Oh.

116
00:12:45,980 --> 00:13:14,050
 The boat, it's turning into Noah's Ark.

117
00:13:14,050 --> 00:13:15,520
 This far.

118
00:13:15,520 --> 00:13:18,590
 The Indians gave us this as gifts from tame toucans.

119
00:13:18,590 --> 00:13:19,290
 Mmm.

120
00:13:19,290 --> 00:13:35,500
 Pardon.

121
00:13:35,500 --> 00:13:40,730
 Those toucans are becoming good parishioners, Padre.

122
00:13:40,730 --> 00:13:41,230
 Haha.

123
00:13:41,230 --> 00:13:55,570
 I will headache.

124
00:13:55,570 --> 00:13:56,930
 My head is bursting.

125
00:13:56,930 --> 00:13:58,230
 Malaria.

126
00:13:58,230 --> 00:14:00,790
 All those mosquitoes, they carry the disease.

127
00:14:00,790 --> 00:14:03,550
 We'll return to Angostura. You'll be alright.

128
00:14:03,550 --> 00:14:17,370
 Quickly, prepare the quinine.

129
00:14:17,370 --> 00:14:28,110
 Drink this.

130
00:14:28,110 --> 00:14:33,110
 This is quinine, just what you need. Drink it up.

131
00:14:33,110 --> 00:14:34,110
 How's Bonplan?

132
00:14:34,110 --> 00:14:35,710
 He's no better, alas.

133
00:14:35,710 --> 00:14:43,550
 Senor, your friend, and Bonplan, you mean.

134
00:14:43,550 --> 00:14:45,770
 Si, senor. He does not move anymore.

135
00:14:45,770 --> 00:14:50,910
 Oh, I'll go. Bonplan.

136
00:14:50,910 --> 00:14:54,490
 Reaction in the pupil, his heart is beating, he's alive!

137
00:14:54,490 --> 00:14:56,190
 More quinine!

138
00:14:56,190 --> 00:15:05,370
 There. A good sign.

139
00:15:05,370 --> 00:15:29,660
 Better now. We thought we were going to lose you.

140
00:15:29,660 --> 00:15:32,240
 I feel better, Padre, only I would like...

141
00:15:32,240 --> 00:15:33,860
 Yes, some more quidines.

142
00:15:33,940 --> 00:15:34,690
 Oh, not exactly.

143
00:15:36,400 --> 00:15:37,500
 Home, if it's possible.

144
00:15:38,220 --> 00:15:38,810
 Carlos?

145
00:15:39,870 --> 00:15:41,810
 You called me, senor?

146
00:15:42,090 --> 00:15:43,350
 Would you please shave me?

147
00:16:06,110 --> 00:16:23,070
 I'm here to pray, but I'm also looking for someone.

148
00:16:23,970 --> 00:16:24,370
 Someone?

149
00:16:25,230 --> 00:16:28,310
 The young lady has returned to her village upriver.

150
00:16:28,570 --> 00:16:30,050
 Oh, I'm much obliged, Padre.

151
00:16:31,400 --> 00:16:33,780
 Senor, she is upriver at her home.

152
00:16:34,190 --> 00:16:35,360
 I know, but I love her.

153
00:16:35,420 --> 00:16:37,240
 It's impossible to go on living without her.

154
00:16:38,570 --> 00:16:39,050
 Senor!

155
00:16:42,230 --> 00:16:48,230
 It's incredible, it's totally unknown.

156
00:16:48,490 --> 00:16:49,650
 A mushroom big as that.

157
00:16:50,030 --> 00:16:51,080
 It's incredible!

158
00:16:51,080 --> 00:17:11,810
 A macrolepidopterus, another species, totally unknown. It's fantastic. That is a Denrobatus, but with many stripes, completely new.

159
00:17:16,620 --> 00:17:21,370
 Well, Carlos, do you think he'll ever come back?

160
00:17:21,680 --> 00:17:25,600
 Well, it's sure, senor. He will return with his arms full of new discoveries.

161
00:17:28,410 --> 00:17:33,010
 Xander, look at this butterfly and how about this frog? They're remarkable.

162
00:17:33,540 --> 00:17:36,700
 Good for you, my friend. You are finding everything that you're looking for.

163
00:17:42,630 --> 00:17:46,050
 Go in peace, my children. You both deserve some good rest.

164
00:17:46,050 --> 00:17:48,950
 More than 2,000 kilometers in this strange land.

165
00:17:48,960 --> 00:17:50,610
 But now, much less strange.

166
00:17:50,850 --> 00:17:56,010
 Yes, and having the company of alligators and of boar constrictors, vampire bats, is hardly ever boring here.

167
00:18:02,030 --> 00:18:13,140
 Delivery of 60 slaves, everyone in excellent health.

168
00:18:13,560 --> 00:18:14,180
 Trouble is tenable, died on the way forward.

169
00:18:14,180 --> 00:18:16,640
 You should be ashamed. They have the right to be free.

170
00:18:16,940 --> 00:18:18,420
 Every human being is free.

171
00:18:19,130 --> 00:18:21,360
 What? Who does he...

172
00:18:21,360 --> 00:18:23,150
 Yes, who does he think he is?

173
00:18:23,210 --> 00:18:25,430
 We just couldn't live here without slaves.

174
00:18:25,570 --> 00:18:27,510
 Everybody knows that blacks aren't human.

175
00:18:28,770 --> 00:18:32,150
 I agree. Slavery is something we must never allow.

176
00:18:32,250 --> 00:18:33,430
 It must be stopped, of course.

177
00:18:33,850 --> 00:18:35,980
 Can we do? After all, we're just two.

178
00:18:35,980 --> 00:18:41,030
 I don't know. We'll leave this country. Go someplace else to Bogota.

179
00:18:42,290 --> 00:18:43,990
 There we can explore the Andes.

180
00:18:44,840 --> 00:18:47,890
 Andes, the Andes, the Andes.

181
00:18:48,340 --> 00:18:50,300
 The world's longest mountain chain.

182
00:18:51,290 --> 00:18:55,090
 7,000 kilometers long, the peaks are more than 6,000 meters up.

183
00:18:55,230 --> 00:18:57,790
 Snow that never melts, volcanoes always erupting.

184
00:18:58,070 --> 00:19:03,670
 So, leaving from Quito, the ancient Inca capital, 2,500 meters above sea level,

185
00:19:03,750 --> 00:19:06,750
 our friend set off to explore the Andes Mountains.

186
00:19:24,430 --> 00:19:38,750
 And there'll never have a man or any living thing.

187
00:19:38,750 --> 00:19:40,470
 They feed on dead carcasses.

188
00:19:40,470 --> 00:19:41,470
 They're scavengers.

189
00:19:41,470 --> 00:19:42,430
 Oh, yuck.

190
00:19:42,430 --> 00:19:44,030
 Oh, I mustn't think bad of them.

191
00:19:44,030 --> 00:19:45,230
 They keep everything clean.

192
00:19:45,230 --> 00:19:51,450
 Look, Borazzo.

193
00:19:51,450 --> 00:19:54,650
 Goes up 6,000 meters, a mountain no one's been able to climb.

194
00:19:54,650 --> 00:19:55,680
 No wonder.

195
00:19:55,680 --> 00:19:57,310
 Much too high to climb.

196
00:19:57,310 --> 00:19:59,340
 We will start our ascent tomorrow.

197
00:19:59,340 --> 00:20:00,450
 Hey, who's we?

198
00:20:00,450 --> 00:20:08,540
 Go no further.

199
00:20:08,540 --> 00:20:10,720
 But without you, we can't go up.

200
00:20:10,720 --> 00:20:12,080
 This mountain's sacred.

201
00:20:12,080 --> 00:20:13,660
 Indians cannot go up.

202
00:20:13,660 --> 00:20:14,810
 We stay here.

203
00:20:14,810 --> 00:20:15,900
 The way up?

204
00:20:15,900 --> 00:20:19,160
 Then let's go up.

205
00:20:19,160 --> 00:20:40,090
 Close and feed.

206
00:20:40,090 --> 00:20:41,350
 We will keep going.

207
00:20:41,350 --> 00:21:13,050
 Oxygen.

208
00:21:13,050 --> 00:21:18,330
 It's the low air pressure, the lack of oxygen.

209
00:21:18,650 --> 00:21:20,000
 We're very high.

210
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:22,780
 It causes the bleeding and it's serious. We'll go down.

211
00:21:22,780 --> 00:21:24,890
 Yes, but we're nearly there. Let's go on.

212
00:21:24,890 --> 00:21:27,650
 We could wait and see when we get accustomed to the height.

213
00:21:27,650 --> 00:21:37,150
 Oh, sit up.

214
00:21:37,150 --> 00:21:46,260
 Ah, too bad we're waiting a few hundred meters of the summit.

215
00:21:46,260 --> 00:21:48,150
 Nobody has ever climbed higher.

216
00:21:48,150 --> 00:21:49,560
 The record.

217
00:21:49,560 --> 00:21:53,280
 But now we have to go down.

218
00:21:53,280 --> 00:22:04,330
 It was right here the Inca Emperor at Hualpa

219
00:22:04,330 --> 00:22:07,100
 told the conquistador Pizarro he'd fill up

220
00:22:07,100 --> 00:22:10,590
 a whole room would go this high as he could reach for his freedom.

221
00:22:10,590 --> 00:22:13,510
 An attack if you release him.

222
00:22:13,510 --> 00:22:15,510
 What else?

223
00:22:15,900 --> 00:22:25,400
 Last, here we are. The Pacific Ocean.

224
00:22:27,010 --> 00:22:29,670
 Strange. So few trees about.

225
00:22:29,810 --> 00:22:32,210
 In fact, very little vegetation. I wonder why.

226
00:22:43,200 --> 00:22:50,050
 It's cold. I mean cold.

227
00:23:01,420 --> 00:23:02,380
 Shove off. Let's go.

228
00:23:08,130 --> 00:23:12,600
 See, the water is cold.

229
00:23:13,120 --> 00:23:16,560
 That's why this particular region has little rainfall and is as out as a desert.

230
00:23:16,680 --> 00:23:17,820
 Yes, but what explains it?

231
00:23:17,880 --> 00:23:20,520
 It's because the cold water cools down the air just above it.

232
00:23:20,760 --> 00:23:21,860
 Cold air holds less moisture.

233
00:23:21,860 --> 00:23:24,880
 But when it reaches the land, it warms up and absorbs moisture.

234
00:23:25,160 --> 00:23:27,380
 There is high humidity and this turns into heavy fog.

235
00:23:27,480 --> 00:23:28,060
 So it rains.

236
00:23:28,200 --> 00:23:30,200
 But not before passing over the mountains.

237
00:23:30,510 --> 00:23:33,230
 The human air rises over the mountains, the Andes going to the east.

238
00:23:33,470 --> 00:23:35,210
 On the other side is where it reigns.

239
00:23:36,560 --> 00:23:41,840
 Washington, the brand new capital of the United States, which has just won its independence.

240
00:23:48,490 --> 00:23:51,670
 All rise for Thomas Jefferson, President of the United States of America.

241
00:23:51,940 --> 00:23:54,720
 Baron von Humboldt, it is a pleasure to welcome you.

242
00:23:55,150 --> 00:23:58,210
 I've heard much about your explorations and your good work.

243
00:23:59,080 --> 00:24:00,890
 And of yours too, Monsieur Bonpland.

244
00:24:01,700 --> 00:24:06,080
 Mr. President, the countries of South America are about to be free and democratic, just as your country.

245
00:24:06,080 --> 00:24:09,660
 But a problem still has to be resolved, sir.

246
00:24:11,310 --> 00:24:13,050
 And what is this problem?

247
00:24:15,570 --> 00:24:18,650
 An enormous problem, Mr. President.

248
00:24:19,270 --> 00:24:19,750
 Slavery.

249
00:24:20,090 --> 00:24:25,450
 You cannot maintain your democracy when you put human beings in slavery because they have black skin.

250
00:24:42,500 --> 00:24:46,440
 The people of Berlin are rising up against injustice, shooting in the streets.

251
00:24:46,560 --> 00:24:47,460
 That's right, Karl.

252
00:24:47,730 --> 00:24:50,360
 There are still some ready to die for liberty.

253
00:24:50,660 --> 00:24:51,580
 I want to see this.

254
00:24:52,020 --> 00:24:56,050
 At the age of 90, Humboldt completed the last volume of his great work, Cosmos.

255
00:24:56,050 --> 00:25:00,390
 He tried to explain that all the forces and physical parts of the world come together to form a unity.

256
00:25:00,900 --> 00:25:03,540
 What causes winds and currents is just a part of the whole.

